---
title: Air-fryer potato wedges
description: Lesson 8.2. An air fryer is a small, fierce convection oven, and should be used like one.
servings: 2
time: 35 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, mastering-the-machines]
lesson: "8.2"
technique: air frying
status: draft
---

> Technique: air frying. An air fryer does not fry. It is a small
> convection oven that blows hot air hard around the food. Everything
> that helps it comes down to one thing: letting that air reach every
> surface.

Cut @potatoes{500%g}(floury, scrubbed, skin on) into wedges about 2 cm thick at the back. Pat them completely dry with a clean tea towel.

> Why dry: a dry surface browns more quickly than a wet one.
-- source: Eden Faithfull, "I Just Got an Air Fryer. Now What?", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-01-12, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/4254-i-just-got-an-air-fryer-now-what

In a #bowl{}, toss the wedges with @oil{2%tsp}(sunflower or rapeseed), @salt{1/2%tsp} and @paprika{1/2%tsp} until evenly coated.

> Why a little oil: it conducts heat to the surface of the food and
> gives good browning and crisping. A few teaspoons is generally
> enough.
-- source: Eden Faithfull, "I Just Got an Air Fryer. Now What?", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-01-12, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/4254-i-just-got-an-air-fryer-now-what ; Scott Kathan, "5 Tips for Better Air Frying", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-01-06, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/6745-5-tips-for-better-air-frying

Spread the wedges in the basket of an #air fryer{} in one layer with gaps between them. If they do not fit, cook two batches.

> Why not fill the basket: an air fryer works by circulating hot air,
> and air needs room to move. An overfilled basket gives poor browning
> and uneven cooking, and the food turns soggy.
-- source: Scott Kathan, "5 Tips for Better Air Frying", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-01-06, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/6745-5-tips-for-better-air-frying ; Eden Faithfull, "I Just Got an Air Fryer. Now What?", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-01-12, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/4254-i-just-got-an-air-fryer-now-what

Cook at 200 °C for ~{10%minutes}. Shake the basket to turn the wedges, then cook for about ~{10%minutes} more, checking after 7, until they are deep gold and a knife goes in easily.

> Why shake, and why check early: most foods need one flip or shake
> halfway so every side meets the air. Air fryers cook faster than
> ovens and differ from each other, so check at the low end of any
> time you are given.
-- source: Scott Kathan, "5 Tips for Better Air Frying", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-01-06, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/6745-5-tips-for-better-air-frying ; "Air Fryer 101", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/lessons/articles/434-air-fryer-101

> Science: it is the oven's rule, made stronger. Water cannot rise
> above 100 °C until it has turned to vapour, so a wet surface cannot
> brown until it has dried. An air fryer circulates hot air, which
> keeps replacing the air at the surface of the food. The test
> kitchen's verdict on the result: closer to oven-fried than
> deep-fried. Look up: forced convection, latent heat of vaporization,
> Maillard reaction.
-- source: Harold McGee, On Food and Cooking, 2nd ed., Scribner, 2004, section "High Temperatures and Dry Cooking Methods", https://app.ckbk.com/reference/food00011c14s001e001se003/high-temperatures-and-dry-cooking-methods ; "Air Fryer 101", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/lessons/articles/434-air-fryer-101 ("essentially countertop convection ovens"; "more often taste oven-fried than deep-fried")

> Next time, without the recipe: dry the food, coat it lightly in oil,
> give it space, shake it halfway, and start checking early. Treat an
> oven recipe's time as too long.
